<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mohammad Reza Pahlavi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1919">1919</a>–<a href="/years/1980">1980</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Mohammad Reza Shah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mohammad Reza <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Pahlavi</a> was the last shah of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, reigning from 1941, when the Anglo-Soviet invasion forced his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reza-shah">Reza Shah</a> to abdicate, until 1979. Restored to full power by the 1953 coup against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-mosaddegh">Mohammad Mosaddegh</a>, he launched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-revolution">White Revolution</a> of land reform and modernisation in 1963 and ruled with the help of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savak">SAVAK</a> secret police. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-revolution">Islamic Revolution</a> drove him into exile in 1979.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mohammad-reza-pahlavi.jpg" alt="Mohammad Reza Pahlavi" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ghazarians · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mohammad_Reza_Pahlavi_1973_portrait_(4x5_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1963 <b>Mohammad Reza Shah</b>’s White Revolution gave Iranian women the vote, and during the revolution of 1979 many of the rights the women’s movement had gained, such as the Family Protection Law, were systematically abolished.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/feminism">Feminism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>Rulers have come to see it: <b>Mohammad Reza Pahlavi</b> with Emperor Haile Selassie in 1968, and Queen Juliana and Prince Bernhard the next year.</blockquote><a href="/work/rock-hewn-churches-of-lalibela">Rock-hewn churches of Lalibela · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Under <b>Mohammad Reza Shah</b> its themes of regicide and the incompetence of kings sat badly with the monarchy, and Ali Shariati opposed it for verses critical of Islam.</blockquote><a href="/work/shahnameh">Shahnameh · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>The Anglo-Soviet invasion of 1941 forced his abdication in favour of his son <b>Mohammad Reza Shah</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/pahlavi-iran">Pahlavi Iran</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in Cairo, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.69,51.39&amp;year=1958" aria-label="Tehran on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5848 874 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Mohammad Reza Pahlavi"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6298 1144h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6298 1144h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.69,51.39&amp;year=1958">What was here in 1958? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
